WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:04.820
Wormable prompt injection becomes more serious when the model is part of an
agent.

2
00:00:04.900 --> 00:00:12.980
An agent may plan steps, call tools, browse, send messages, edit records,
update files, or trigger workflows.

3
00:00:13.060 --> 00:00:19.860
The model is no longer only generating text for a human to read. It may be
influencing operations across connected systems.

4
00:00:19.940 --> 00:00:32.180
The core design mistake is excessive agency. If the agent has broad
credentials, broad tool access, write permissions, and unattended execution,
prompt injection can become an authorization problem.

5
00:00:32.260 --> 00:00:37.460
The model may not understand the security boundary, but the application
still gave it a way to cross that boundary.

6
00:00:37.540 --> 00:00:44.660
OWASP emphasizes least privilege and human approval for high-risk actions
because the surrounding system must compensate for model uncertainty.

7
00:00:44.740 --> 00:00:49.220
A connector should be treated like an API surface, not like a harmless
convenience feature.

8
00:00:49.300 --> 00:01:01.220
Email, calendar, storage, source control, ticketing, CRM, cloud, chat, and
browser connectors all carry different consequences. Reading a page is not
the same as sending a message.

9
00:01:01.300 --> 00:01:07.860
Drafting a recommendation is not the same as submitting a transaction.
Permission scopes should reflect those differences.

10
00:01:07.940 --> 00:01:18.260
Blast radius can be reduced with separate identities, narrow tokens,
allowlisted tools, action-specific policies, rate limits, destination
controls, and approval gates.

11
00:01:18.340 --> 00:01:25.380
A low-risk summarization task should not run with the same authority as a
workflow that can message customers or change production configuration.

12
00:01:25.460 --> 00:01:30.340
When possible, split read, draft, and execute privileges into different
steps.

13
00:01:30.420 --> 00:01:37.700
Review connector permissions by use case, not by product category, because
the same connector may be low risk in one workflow and high risk in another.

14
00:01:37.780 --> 00:01:47.380
The safest agent designs assume the model can be influenced by untrusted
context. They place meaningful controls around what the model can observe,
decide, and do.

15
00:01:47.460 --> 00:01:53.220
That is how a prompt injection becomes a contained error instead of a
cross-system propagation event.

16
00:01:53.300 --> 00:02:01.380
The goal is not to remove useful automation; it is to make automation
proportional to the trustworthiness of its inputs and the consequence of its
actions.
